<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#deutsche-mark">Glossary</a> · Term · currency</p><h1 class="enc-title">Deutsche Mark</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1948">1948</a>–<a href="/years/2001">2001</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Deutsche Mark was the currency of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-germany">West Germany</a> from June 1948 and of reunified <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> from 1990 until it was replaced by the euro in 2002. Its introduction in the western zones of occupied Germany helped end the black market and prompted the Soviet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">blockade</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-berlin">West Berlin</a>. Montenegro adopted it in 1999 as a stable substitute for the Yugoslav dinar.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/deutsche-mark.jpg" alt="Deutsche Mark" width="320" height="155" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Deutsche Bundesbank, Frankfurt am Main, Germany · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:50_DM_1996.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
